# Line-ending policy.
#
# Why this file exists: without it, Git for Windows applies its installer
# default `core.autocrlf=true` and rewrites every checked-out text file to
# CRLF. About 35 tests assert on the RAW SOURCE TEXT of electron/main.ts with
# `\n` anchors — electron/main.accountAuthStateWiring.test.ts (20),
# electron/main.mailRulesWiring.test.ts (12), electron/main.backgroundMail.test.ts
# (3) — and every one of them fails on `\r\n`. A fresh Windows clone was red out
# of the box; re-cloning with `core.eol=lf` turned them green. Measured on the
# Windows stand 2026-08-27, not inferred.
#
# NOTE — the index and this policy do NOT yet agree. `git ls-files --eol`
# reports 21 blobs stored as `i/crlf` plus 1 `i/mixed` (tsconfig.json,
# tsconfig.node.json, .releaserc.json, .commitlintrc.json, src/vite-env.d.ts,
# electron/logger.test.ts, electron/sentry.test.ts and the docs legal pages).
# This file governs how the working tree is MATERIALISED; it does not rewrite
# what is already stored. Until a deliberate `git add --renormalize .` lands in
# its own commit, a `git add` touching one of those files can produce a
# whole-file diff. That renormalisation is a pending decision, not an oversight.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts are run by bash; a CRLF shebang line makes the interpreter
# lookup fail with a confusing "bad interpreter: no such file or directory".
*.sh text eol=lf

# Windows-native batch files are the one place CRLF is correct.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary assets must never go through any line-ending filter.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.asar binary
*.node binary
